00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.739
Leah: What is Test Driven Development?

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:07.350
The Test Driven Development approach
creates test cases to specify and

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.020
validate what the code will do.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:14.430
Test cases for each function are ‘created
and tested first’ before the actual

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:19.139
development of the application or program
and if the test fails, new code is written.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.779
Chris: TDD is based on 3
main principles which are:

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:23.020
1.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:28.770
Do not write any production code unless it
is to make a ‘failing unit test’ ‘pass’.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.130
2.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:34.390
Do not write any more of a ‘unit
test’ than is sufficient to ‘fail’.

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:35.249
3.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.780
Do not write any more production code than is
sufficient to ‘pass’ the ‘one failing unit test’.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:46.110
Leah: Since points 1 and 3 are both
involve “refactoring” of code, this can

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.730
be simplified to ‘2 points’ which are:

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:49.450
1.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.640
Write only enough of a unit test to fail.

00:00:52.109 --> 00:00:52.299
2.

00:00:52.299 --> 00:00:56.000
Write only enough production code
to make the failing unit test, pass.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.680
So how is this done in practice?

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:00.760
Chris: Step 1

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.629
A developer writes the ‘test case’
without any code in front of them.

00:01:05.139 --> 00:01:10.440
This may sound tricky, but it ensures that the
developer understands the functionality required.

00:01:11.109 --> 00:01:17.200
The test case is not biased to show the ‘code’
works, rather to test the ‘expected feature’.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:22.180
In this step you will try to ‘run the
test’ and as the ‘actual code’ has not

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.689
been written, your test will ‘fail’.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:26.360
Leah: Step 2

00:01:26.389 --> 00:01:29.509
The developer writes only the
minimum required code, to pass the

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.830
test case, that just ‘got failed’.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.050
This becomes a challenging activity
for developers who are used to writing

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.350
code for the ‘full module’ in one go.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.149
The “Just enough” code concept, helps in
ensuring that no extra bit of code is added.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.829
The developer again runs the test
case, and make sure it passes.

00:01:49.389 --> 00:01:50.270
Step 3

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:51.491
Chris: The developers ‘refactor’ the
code, to ensure the functionality

00:01:51.491 --> 00:01:52.320
is intact and code is refined.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:02:02.809
In steps 1 and 2 the focus is to write code
only, whereas other important points for

00:02:02.809 --> 00:02:05.520
efficient design, maintainable code etc.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:06.580
are not considered.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:12.750
Once the test ‘passes’ the developer will check
for any ‘unnecessary code’ which can be removed.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:18.230
Leah: While TDD may seem like it adds
extra work to the development of software,

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:22.600
it constrains the complexity and effort
which is put into the initial coding.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.380
Chris: This comes with several benefits such as:

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.609
• Reducing rework time and enhancing productivity.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.430
• Allowing for quick exploration of bugs and errors.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.730
• Leah: Making it quick to get feedback,
and encouraging knowledge sharing and

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:42.259
collaboration, so that anyone should be
able to make a start on any section of code.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:47.120
• Allowing programmers to easily change
overarching program architecture and

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.000
encouraging cleaner program design.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.970
• Chris: It results in the creation of extensive
code that is flexible and easy to maintain.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:02.009
• It also means that requirements in the design
spec are addressed directly during implementation.

